271:\title[Cycle decompositions in $3$-uniform hypergraphs]{Cycle decompositions in $3$-uniform hypergraphs}
281:\keywords{Hypergraphs, Euler tours, cycles}
284:	We show that $3$-graphs on $n$ vertices whose codegree is at least $(2/3 + o(1))n$ can be decomposed into tight cycles and admit Euler tours, subject to the trivial necessary divisibility conditions.
285:	We also provide a construction showing that our bounds are best possible up to the $o(1)$ term.
293:\subsection{Cycle decompositions}
295:Given a~$k$-uniform hypergraph~$H$, a~\emph{decomposition of~$H$} is a collection of subgraphs of $H$ such that every edge of~$H$ is covered exactly once.
296:When these subgraphs are all isomorphic copies of a single hypergraph~$F$ we say that it is an~\emph{$F$-decomposition}, and that $H$ is \emph{$F$-decomposable}.
297:Finding decompositions of hypergraphs is one of the oldest problems in combinatorics.
298:For instance, the well-known problem of the existence of designs and Steiner systems can be cast as the problem of decomposing a complete hypergraph into smaller complete hypergraphs of a fixed size.
299:Thanks to the recent breakthroughs of Keevash~\cite{Keevash2014} and Glock, Kühn, Lo, and Osthus~\cite{GKLO2020} our knowledge about hypergraph decompositions has increased substantially; but many open questions remain.
302:Here we focus in decompositions in which the subgraphs are all cycles.
303:For $k \ge 2$ and $\ell \geq k+1$, the \textit{$k$-uniform tight cycle of length~$\ell$} is the $k$-graph $C^k_{\ell}$ whose vertices are $\{v_1, v_2, \dots, v_{\ell} \}$ and whose edges are all $k$-sets of consecutive vertices of the form $\{ v_i,v_{i+1}, \dotsc, v_{i+k-1} \}$ for $1 \leq i \leq \ell$, where the indices are understood modulo $\ell$.
304:Since no other kind of hypergraph cycles will be considered, we will refer to tight cycles as \emph{cycles}.
309:Note that in a $k$-uniform cycle every vertex has degree exactly $k$.
310:This implies that, for any $\ell \ge k+1$, any $C^k_{\ell}$-decomposable $k$-graph $H$ must necessarily be $k$-vertex-divisible.
311:Another obvious necessary condition to find $C_{\ell}$-decompositions in $H$ is that the total number of edges of $H$ must be divisible by $\ell$.
314:However, not every $C_{\ell}$-divisible $k$-graph is $C_{\ell}$-decomposable.
315:For instance, a cycle $C_{2\ell}$ is $C_{\ell}$-divisible, but clearly does not have a $C_{\ell}$-decomposition.
316:This motivates the search of easily-checkable sufficient conditions which, together with the necessary $C_{\ell}$-divisibility, already force the existence of $C_{\ell}$-decompositions.
321:The \emph{$C^k_{\ell}$-decomposition threshold} $\delta_{\smash{C^k_{\ell}}}(n)$ is the minimum $d$ such that every $C^k_{\ell}$-divisible $k$-graph $H$ on $n$ vertices with $\delta_{k-1}(H) \geq d$ is $C^k_{\ell}$-decomposable.
328:\begin{theorem} \label{theorem:ldecomposcodegree}
337:Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree} implies an interesting contrast with respect to what is known for $C^2_{\ell}$-decomposition thresholds, which we now recall.
339:Barber, Kühn, Lo, and Osthus~\cite{BKLO2016} introduced the technique of \emph{iterative absorption} to study $F$-decompositions in graphs
341:In particular, for cycle decompositions in graphs, their work implies that $\delta_{\smash{C_{\ell}}}(n) \leq \delta_{\smash{C_{\ell}}}^\ast(n)  + o(n)$.
342:Here, $\delta_{\smash{C_{\ell}}}^\ast(n)$ is the minimum degree which guarantees the existence of `fractional $C_{\ell}$-decompositions' in $n$-vertex graphs.
343:This notion corresponds to the natural fractional relaxation of decompositions (we define and discuss this in Section~\ref{subsection:wellbehaved}).
351:On the other hand, cycles of even length are bipartite, and Glock, Kühn, Lo, Montgomery, and Osthus~\cite{GKLMO2019} were able to characterise the `decomposition thresholds' for all bipartite graphs.
356:In contrast, Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree} implies that for $k = 3$ and large $\ell$ the behaviour is different: $\delta_{\smash{C^3_{\ell}}} = 2/3$ for all $\ell$ sufficiently large, regardless of whether the cycle is tripartite or not.
359:Say a $k$-graph has a \emph{cycle decomposition} if it admits a decomposition into cycles.
360:That is, there are edge-disjoint cycles ---not necessarily of the same length--- which cover every edge exactly once.
361:This notion is weaker than that of having a $C_{\ell}$-decomposition for a fixed $\ell$.
362:It is easy to see that any $3$-graph having a cycle decomposition must be $3$-vertex-divisible.
363:As a corollary of Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree}, we obtain an upper bound on the minimum codegree sufficient to force a cycle decomposition.
365:\begin{cor} \label{theorem:cycledecomposcodegree}
366:    Any $3$-vertex-divisible $3$-graph $H$ with $\delta_2(H) \geq (2/3 + o(1))|H|$ has a cycle decomposition.
369:\subsection{Euler tours}
371:Our focus in decompositions into cycles is partly motivated by its close connections with the celebrated problem of finding \textit{Euler tours}.
373:If a hypergraph~$H$ contains a tour that covers each edge exactly once, we call it~\emph{Euler tour} and we say that~$H$ is \emph{Eulerian}.
375:Famously, Euler~\cite{Euler1741} proved that every Eulerian graph must be~$2$-vertex-divisible, 
376:and stated (later proved by Hierholzer and Wiener~\cite{HierholzerWiener1873}) that connected and $2$-vertex-divisible graphs are Eulerian.
377:Analogously, for $k \geq 3$, it is an easy observation that every Eulerian $k$-graph must be $k$-vertex-divisible.
378:However, the characterisation of Eulerian $k$-graphs is not as simple as for $k = 2$.
379:In fact, until recently, it was not even known if complete $k$-vertex-divisible $k$-graphs were Eulerian.
381:This was proven to be true by Glock, Joos, Kühn, and Osthus~\cite{GJKO2020}, which deduced this from a more general result which finds Euler tours in $k$-graphs with certain quasirandom conditions (which are satisfied by complete graphs).
383:From this more general result, they also deduced a `minimum codegree' version of their theorem: there exists~$c>0$ such that any sufficiently large~$3$-vertex-divisible hypergraph~$H$ with~$\delta_2(H)\geq (1-c)\vert H\vert$ is Eulerian.
385:Their proof is based fundamentally on a reduction to the problem of finding a cycle decomposition.
386:In the same fashion, we can use Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree} to improve the minimum codegree condition.
389:    Any $3$-vertex-divisible $3$-graph $H$ with $\delta_2(H) \geq (2/3 + o(1))|H|$ is Eulerian.
394:Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree}, Corollary~\ref{theorem:cycledecomposcodegree} and Corollary~\ref{corollary:codegreeuler} hold for $3$-graphs $H$ satisfying $\delta_2(H) \geq (2/3 + o(1))|H|$.
395:Glock, Kühn, and Osthus~\cite[Conjecture 5.6]{GlockKuhnOsthus2020} conjectured that Corollary~\ref{theorem:cycledecomposcodegree} should hold already for any $H$ with~$\delta_2(H)\ge (1/2+o(1))\vert H \vert$.
396:Similarly, in the setting of Corollary~\ref{corollary:codegreeuler}, Glock, Joos, Kühn, and Osthus~\cite[Conjecture 3]{GJKO2020} conjectured (reiterated in~\cite[Conjecture 5.4]{GlockKuhnOsthus2020}) that a minimum codegree of~$(1/2+o(1))|H|$ should be enough to guarantee the existence of Euler tours.
399:We prove this by constructing a family of counterexamples which are able to cover all of the previous settings ($C_{\ell}$-decompositions, cycle decompositions, and Euler tours) in a unified way.
401:A \emph{tour decomposition} of $H$ is a collection of edge-disjoint tours in $H$ which, together, cover all edges of $H$.
403:Note that a cycle is precisely a tour which does not repeat vertices.
404:Thus we have that both $C_{\ell}$-decompositions and cycle decompositions are particular instances of tour decompositions,
405:and moreover Eulerian graphs are graphs which admit a tour decomposition consisting of a single tour.
406:Thus the following result shows that Theorem~\ref{theorem:ldecomposcodegree}, Corollary~\ref{theorem:cycledecomposcodegree}, and Corollary~\ref{corollary:codegreeuler} are asymptotically tight for the minimum codegree condition.
